---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Header from '../../components/Header.astro';
import Footer from '../../components/Footer.astro';
import Giscus from '../../components/Giscus.astro';
import CompareFaq from '../../components/CompareFaq.astro';

const lang = 'en' as const;
const FAQ = [
  {
    q: 'Which of the three Personal Cloud Drive E2EE sync modes should I choose?',
    a: '(1) Choose WebDAV E2EE Sync if you use Nextcloud, Synology NAS, Nutstore (坚果云), or AList/OpenList — it syncs per-note over HTTPS without any desktop client. (2) Choose Local Cloud Drive Encrypted Vault Folder if you already have iCloud Drive, OneDrive, Dropbox, Google Drive, Baidu Netdisk, Aliyun Drive, or Quark Drive installed locally — SoloMD writes .enc ciphertext into that synced folder. (3) Choose Direct Cloud Drive OpenAPI Sync if you want a clientless single-bundle encrypted backup (solomd-vault.enc) that hides even your filenames and folder structure on OneDrive, Dropbox, Baidu Netdisk, or Aliyun Drive.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Why does Nutstore (坚果云) return 401 / 403 on WebDAV?',
    a: 'Nutstore requires a dedicated Third-Party App Password rather than your account login password. Go to Nutstore Web → Account Info → Security Options → Third-Party App Management → Add App to generate a password. Use https://dav.jianguoyun.com/dav/ as the server URL, your account email as username, and the generated app password.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can I recover my vault if I forget my E2EE passphrase?',
    a: 'No. SoloMD uses zero-knowledge Argon2id key derivation + XChaCha20-Poly1305 authenticated encryption entirely on your device. Neither cloud providers nor GitHub ever see your plaintext or passphrase. Store your passphrase safely in a password manager.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Why do I get a 401 error when connecting GitHub?',
    a: '401 means GitHub rejected the token: (1) fine-grained tokens expire after 30–90 days by default; (2) you pasted your account password instead of a Personal Access Token; (3) the token was revoked or regenerated; or (4) a network proxy stripped the Authorization header. Generate a fresh PAT with Contents: Read and write (or repo scope) and paste it into Settings → Sync.',
  },
];
---

<Layout
  title="Sync, History & Encrypted Cloud Backup in SoloMD — WebDAV, OneDrive, Dropbox, GitHub & Git"
  description="Set up SoloMD's three zero-knowledge Personal Cloud Drive E2EE sync modes (WebDAV, Local Encrypted Vault Folder, and Direct Cloud OpenAPI), GitHub sync with E2EE, and local Git history snapshots."
  lang={lang}
>
  <Header lang={lang} />
  <main id="main">
    <article class="container docs-article">
      <nav class="breadcrumbs">
        <a href="/">Home</a> / <a href="/docs/">Docs</a> / <span>Sync &amp; History</span>
      </nav>

      <h1>Sync, History &amp; Encrypted Cloud Backup</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        Your notes remain plain <code>.md</code> files on disk — and before anything leaves your device for a cloud drive or remote host, <code>Argon2id + XChaCha20-Poly1305</code> zero-knowledge encryption keeps it private.
      </p>

      <h2>1. Personal Cloud Drive End-to-End Encrypted Sync (3 Modes)</h2>
      <p>
        Open <strong>Settings → Sync</strong> (or <strong>File → Sync &amp; History</strong>) to configure any of the three personal cloud drive sync modes:
      </p>

      <h3>Mode 1: WebDAV End-to-End Encrypted Sync (Nextcloud, Synology NAS, Nutstore, AList)</h3>
      <ol>
        <li>Open <strong>Settings → Sync → WebDAV Sync</strong>.</li>
        <li><strong>Pick a preset or enter your WebDAV server URL</strong>:
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Nextcloud / ownCloud</strong>: <code>https://your-domain/remote.php/dav/files/username/</code></li>
            <li><strong>Nutstore (坚果云)</strong>: <code>https://dav.jianguoyun.com/dav/</code> with your email and a generated <em>Third-Party App Password</em>.</li>
            <li><strong>Synology NAS / AList / OpenList</strong>: your WebDAV endpoint URL (e.g. <code>http://host:5244/dav/</code>).</li>
          </ul>
        </li>
        <li><strong>Remote folder</strong>: e.g. <code>/SoloMD</code>.</li>
        <li><strong>Enable End-to-End Encryption (recommended)</strong>: toggle on E2EE and set an encryption passphrase so every note is encrypted locally before upload.</li>
        <li>Click <strong>Test Connection</strong>, then use <strong>Upload to WebDAV</strong> or <strong>Download from WebDAV</strong> across your devices.</li>
      </ol>

      <h3>Mode 2: Local Cloud Drive Encrypted Vault Folder (OneDrive, iCloud, Dropbox, Google Drive, Baidu, Aliyun, Quark)</h3>
      <p>
        If you already run a desktop cloud drive client that syncs a local folder on your computer:
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li>Open <strong>Settings → Sync → Local Cloud Drive Encrypted Vault</strong>.</li>
        <li>Click <strong>Auto-Detect Cloud Drive Folders</strong> (automatically finds iCloud Drive, OneDrive, Dropbox, Google Drive, Baidu Netdisk, Aliyun Drive, Quark Drive, and Nutstore folders on your machine) or pick any custom synced folder.</li>
        <li>Set your <strong>encryption passphrase</strong>.</li>
        <li>Click <strong>Encrypt &amp; Backup to Vault Folder</strong>: SoloMD encrypts every note in your workspace into <code>.enc</code> ciphertext inside that synced folder, and your cloud client uploads it in the background.</li>
        <li><strong>Restore on another computer</strong>: select the synced vault folder, enter your passphrase, and click <strong>Decrypt &amp; Restore to Workspace</strong>.</li>
      </ol>

      <h3>Mode 3: Direct Cloud Drive OpenAPI Sync (Clientless &amp; Hides Filenames)</h3>
      <p>
        Want cloud backup without installing a desktop sync client — and without exposing your filenames or folder hierarchy to the cloud provider?
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li>Open <strong>Settings → Sync → Direct Cloud Drive OpenAPI</strong>.</li>
        <li>Select your provider: <strong>Microsoft OneDrive (Graph API)</strong>, <strong>Dropbox API</strong>, <strong>Baidu Netdisk OpenAPI</strong>, or <strong>Aliyun Drive OpenAPI</strong>.</li>
        <li>Enter your <strong>Access Token</strong>, remote bundle path (e.g. <code>/apps/SoloMD/solomd-vault.enc</code>), and your <strong>encryption passphrase</strong>.</li>
        <li>Click <strong>Pack, Encrypt &amp; Upload</strong>: SoloMD compresses and encrypts your entire workspace into a single <code>solomd-vault.enc</code> ciphertext bundle and uploads it directly via HTTPS API. Even your note titles, count, and directory tree remain completely hidden.</li>
        <li>On any other device, enter the same token and passphrase and click <strong>Download &amp; Decrypt to Workspace</strong>.</li>
      </ol>

      <h2>2. Local Git History (Automatic Snapshots &amp; One-Click Rollback)</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Go to <strong>Settings → History → Initialize Git History</strong> to turn your workspace into a local Git repository — no account required, nothing leaves your machine.</li>
        <li>Turn on <strong>Auto-commit on save</strong> so every save creates a snapshot, or trigger “Save Snapshot Now” anytime.</li>
        <li>Open the <strong>History</strong> pane in the right sidebar to inspect the per-note timeline, view diffs, or roll back to any snapshot in one click.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>3. GitHub Sync (with Optional E2EE)</h2>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Create a Personal Access Token</strong> at <code>github.com → Settings → Developer settings → Personal access tokens</code> (Fine-grained with <strong>Contents: Read and write</strong>, or Classic with <strong><code>repo</code></strong> scope).</li>
        <li>Paste the token in <strong>Settings → Sync → GitHub</strong> and link an existing repo or let SoloMD create a private vault repository.</li>
        <li><strong>Optional E2EE</strong>: enable a passphrase so SoloMD pushes an encrypted shadow copy to GitHub instead of plaintext.</li>
      </ol>

      <h2>4. Mobile Sync (iCloud / Android SAF / Syncthing)</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>iPhone / iPad + Mac (iCloud)</strong>: place your notes folder in iCloud Drive and open the same folder via the Files app on iOS/iPadOS (see <a href="/docs/icloud/">Sync with iCloud</a>).</li>
        <li><strong>Android + Desktop (Syncthing / FolderSync)</strong>: grant folder access via Android’s system folder picker; SoloMD automatically reloads externally synced changes when you return to the app.</li>
      </ul>

      <CompareFaq heading="Frequently asked questions" items={FAQ} />
      <Giscus />
    </article>
  </main>
  <Footer lang={lang} />
</Layout>
